# Direct dependencies -- the ONLY hand-edited dependency file in this tree.
#
# Do not add pins here for libraries the application does not import directly:
# that is what requirements.lock is for. Edit this file, then regenerate:
#
#     pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-emit-index-url --no-emit-trusted-host \
#         --allow-unsafe --output-file=requirements.lock requirements.in
#
# TIER-2 cycle 3 (BUG-6): `--allow-unsafe` is REQUIRED, not decoration, and
# omitting it produces a lock that cannot be installed. pip-tools depends on
# `setuptools` and `pip`, which pip-compile classifies as "unsafe" and leaves
# OUT of the lock by default -- while `--generate-hashes` puts pip in
# `--require-hashes` mode, where every requirement must be pinned with `==`.
# With pip-tools in this file and `--allow-unsafe` absent, the lock shipped
# `pip-tools==7.6.1` with an UNPINNED `setuptools` beside it, and
# `pip install -r requirements.lock` in a fresh venv exited 1:
#   ERROR: In --require-hashes mode, all requirements must have their versions
#   pinned with ==. These do not: setuptools ...
# `--allow-unsafe` puts pip, setuptools and wheel into the lock WITH `==` pins
# and hashes, which is the only form that both keeps them hash-verified and
# installable. Removing the flag reintroduces a lock that does not install.
#
# TIER-2 cycle 2 (BUG-5): pip-compile is the GENERATOR of requirements.lock and
# was documented here, in requirements.txt and in the lock's own header while
# being pinned in none of them -- so the file that decides every transitive
# version had no declared version. pip-tools is pinned below for the same
# reason ruff is: its output is what CI installs, and a floating generator
# resolves a different set each time it runs. The three command lines that
# document this now agree; the lock's own header still prints a `--no-index`
# that pip-compile adds to its internal invocation and that this command does
# NOT pass, so do not copy the header -- copy this one. (The header line is
# generated, not hand-written; it is recorded here so the discrepancy is not
# rediscovered as a mystery.)
#
# requirements.txt is a POINTER to that lock (it contains `-r requirements.lock`
# and nothing else), so `pip install -r requirements.txt` and
# `pip install -r requirements.lock` install byte-identical sets, and there is
# exactly one file whose contents a machine wrote. requirements.lock is
# committed on purpose: an unpinned transitive is a dependency nobody chose,
# resolved at install time, and it is the reason a green suite can go red on a
# commit that changed no Python.
#
# The `==` pins below are deliberate and load-bearing, so they stay in the .in
# rather than floating: each one is a version-sensitive decision, and the reason
# is written next to it. Dropping a pin here and letting the compiler choose
# would make those decisions silently.
Django==6.1.1
# P1-5 (2026-10-24): 3.18.2 over 3.18.1. The patch release is the fixed version
# for the three HIGH advisories the dependency gate reports against 3.18.1
# (CVE-2026-114136/114137/114138); it stays inside the same 3.18 line, so no
# serializer, view or exception behaviour changes.
djangorestframework==3.18.2
djangorestframework-simplejwt==5.5.1
django-cors-headers==4.9.0
# SPEC-20-9: scannable MFA enrollment QR from the otpauth:// provisioning
# URI (common/qr.py). Pure Python, no runtime dependencies, emits SVG
# without an image library.
segno==1.6.1
razorpay==2.0.1
# SPEC-22-01: production Postgres. Django's postgresql backend loads psycopg3
# at first connection, so without this the documented postgres DATABASE_URL
# crashes the moment prod takes its first query. [binary] ships prebuilt
# libpq wheels (no compiler needed in the image).
psycopg[binary]==3.3.6
# SPEC-22-04 [R-22.13]: error tracking, imported ONLY when SENTRY_DSN is set
# (config/settings.py _init_error_tracking). Without a DSN the package is
# never imported, so the app boots and behaves exactly as it did before it
# was added - which is also why the test suite never needs it: the SDK init
# is stubbed. Official SDK, pinned like everything else here.
sentry-sdk==2.71.0
# WSGI server for config.wsgi (the Dockerfile CMD); Django's dev server is
# not a production server.
gunicorn==26.2.0
# SPEC-2-03 / SPEC-19-1 (ASYNC-INFRA): celery, the task queue and the beat
# scheduler the repo LACKED. Nothing scheduled expire_reservations,
# drain_notification_outbox or purge_notification_outbox before this pin, so
# reservations never expired and the notification outbox was never drained -
# a real audit finding, not a hypothetical. Pinned like everything else here
# because the resolved TRANSITIVE set (kombu, amqp, billiard, vine, the
# click-* chain and its re-exports, prompt_toolkit, python-dateutil, six,
# tzlocal, wcwidth) is what CI installs, and a floating celery resolves a
# different one each time it runs.
celery==5.6.3
# The Redis protocol client, pinned as a DIRECT dependency rather than left
# implicit in celery's extras, for the same reason the broker URL is
# env-driven rather than hardcoded: config.settings hands it to celery as the
# broker and result-backend transport, so this package is on the worker's
# import path for the worker to boot at all. The default broker being Redis is
# not a constraint - a deployment pointing CELERY_BROKER_URL elsewhere installs
# that broker's own client.
redis==8.1.0
# Serves STATIC_ROOT from the app process, so DEBUG=false deployments still
# ship admin CSS/JS without a separate web server or object store.
whitenoise==6.11.0
python-dotenv==1.2.3
# TIER1-S3-MEDIA: the storage backend behind the DJANGO_MEDIA_BACKEND seam
# (SPEC-2-04 [V-13]) - Cloudflare R2 preferred, AWS S3 the alternative, ONE
# implementation: django-storages' S3Storage, which is S3-compatible and
# honours a custom endpoint_url, so either provider is pure configuration
# (settings._env_media_storage_options reads the AWS_* keys; no bucket,
# credential or endpoint is written into this repository - white-label). The
# [s3] extra pulls boto3 (the S3 client) with it; boto3 is deliberately NOT
# pinned directly, because application code never imports it - storages
# does - unlike redis, which celery needs on ITS import path to boot. Pinned
# like everything else here: the storage behaviour (URL signing, upload
# batching, credential lookup) is version-sensitive, and a floating pin here
# would change what production uploads do without a commit.
django-storages[s3]==1.14.6
# P1-5 (2026-10-24): 13.0.0 over 12.3.0. 12.3.0 is flagged CRITICAL and HIGH by
# the dependency gate; 13.0.0 is the first release that clears that set. This
# app only exercises Pillow through ImageField validation (no direct Image
# manipulation), and the suite's upload pins cover that path.
Pillow==13.0.0
coverage==7.16.1
# GATE-1 (2026-10-04): ruff is both the linter and the FORMATTER OF RECORD
# for this tree (config: backend/pyproject.toml; gate: the `lint` job in
# .github/workflows/backend-tests.yml). Pinned like everything else above
# because formatter output is version-sensitive -- the pin is what makes a
# local run and a CI run agree byte for byte, and dropping it would let the
# gate silently start reformatting on whatever ruff is current.
ruff==0.16.10
# TIER-2 item 3: pre-commit, the runner for .pre-commit-config.yaml (ruff +
# trailing-whitespace + check-yaml, all on pinned revs). A DEVELOPMENT tool,
# not a runtime one, but it is pinned here for the same reason ruff is: the
# hooks' behaviour is version-sensitive, and an unpinned runner is how a
# commit passes locally and fails in CI. It resolves into requirements.lock like
# every other pin, so the CI step installs it from the same resolved set.
pre-commit==4.6.2
# TIER-2 item 4: mypy, scoped by [tool.mypy] in backend/pyproject.toml to
# common/ and orders/state.py. Pinned like everything else here: a type
# checker's findings are version-sensitive in exactly the way a formatter's
# are, and a floating pin would let the gate start reporting on whatever mypy
# happens to be current.
mypy==2.4.0
# TIER-2 cycle 2 (BUG-5): pip-tools, which PROVIDES the `pip-compile` that
# generates requirements.lock. Pinned here for the same reason everything else
# on this list is: the lock is the file CI installs from, so the tool that
# writes it is part of the dependency contract -- an unpinned generator resolves
# a different transitive set every time it is run, which is the exact failure
# mode the lock exists to prevent, reintroduced one level up.
pip-tools==7.6.1
# MUT-1: mutmut, the automated mutation runner behind
# .github/workflows/mutation-weekly.yml. Weekly and workflow_dispatch only --
# it never runs on a pull_request, so it cannot gate one.
#
# 2.5.1 and NOT the current 3.8.0, and the reason is not age. mutmut 3 takes
# `pytest>=6.2.5` as a HARD requirement (its own metadata), and this repo's
# floor is measured by `manage.py test`; adopting 3.x would mean carrying a
# second test runner whose result is not the number the changelog prints.
# 2.5.1 takes any command as its runner, so the project's own runner is used
# unchanged. Its base install is also pytest-free (pytest is only an extra).
#
# Config is [tool.mutmut] in backend/pyproject.toml. Two gotchas measured while
# wiring this up, both of which cost a run before they were understood:
#   - `runner` IS read from that config file. mutmut declares `--runner` with
#     no default, so the value click passes is None (falsy) and the config
#     merge substitutes the file's value; the non-empty pytest runner is only
#     that merge's Python fallback. `--runner` on the command line is therefore
#     an OVERRIDE of the file's value, not a requirement. paths_to_mutate and
#     the boolean flags below are read from the file for the same reason: their
#     defaults are falsy too.
#   - `simple_output` does not disable the progress spinner; `no_progress`
#     does. With only simple_output set, mutmut dies on a cp1252 console with
#     UnicodeEncodeError on the braille character it writes to stdout.
mutmut==2.5.1